WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:03.260
Hello, and welcome to our webcast,

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:06.080
MBA Students Insights: Life Outside Tech
at the GSB.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.071
My name is Jason Dunford.

00:00:08.071 --> 00:00:10.820
And I'm a first-year MBA
student here at the GSB.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.830
And I'm joined by JB Monu and
Jessica Holton.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.730
Like me, they are pursuing career
interests outside the tech sector.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.647
So today we'll be talking a little bit
about the student experience here at

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:25.583
the GSB and we'll answer as many
of your questions as we can.

00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:28.804
So to submit a question to us about
what it's like to be a student,

00:00:28.804 --> 00:00:31.382
just click the Chat button
at the top of your screen,

00:00:31.382 --> 00:00:34.160
enter your question, and
click Send to all Panelists.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:40.740
Please be aware that we won't be taking
any questions about the admission process,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.750
the application, or financial aid today.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.719
You can consult the website or
contact the MBA admissions office for

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:48.908
that information.

00:00:48.908 --> 00:00:50.340
All right, let's get started.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.800
We'll begin with introductions,
where we're from, what we were each

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.130
doing before coming to GSB, and
what our goals are for after graduation.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.750
So I'll go ahead and start.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.130
Again, my name's Jason Dunford.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:05.580
I grew up in Kenya, came to the US for
college about ten years ago.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.260
I was an international swimmer,
so I swam during college and

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.020
then beyond college representing Kenya.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.517
And then after that I worked
a couple of jobs before the GSB.

00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:21.149
The first one was in electronics recycling
and then after that I moved into the solar

00:01:21.149 --> 00:01:24.657
industry, and I became very
passionate about clean energy and

00:01:24.657 --> 00:01:27.600
see myself working in that
industry beyond the GSB.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.115
Now I'll turn it over to JB.

00:01:30.115 --> 00:01:31.710
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm JB Monu.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:36.240
I grew up in Upstate New York, but most
recently I've been living in Los Angeles.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.010
Prior to the GSB,
I worked in the entertainment industry.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:43.250
So I spent four and a half years
at a talent agency, Entourage, and

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.080
a year and
a half at a TV production company.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.389
And I'm looking to get into
management consulting, post-GSB.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:51.480
Thanks, JB, Jessica?

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.180
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone, I'm Jessica,
it's great to be with you here today.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.540
I grew up on the East Coast, primarily New
Jersey and most recently from New York.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.445
And prior to the GSB, I was in finance.

00:02:01.445 --> 00:02:03.758
I did two years of investment banking and

00:02:03.758 --> 00:02:07.577
two years of private equity,
focusing on the consumer industry.

00:02:07.577 --> 00:02:12.526
And post-graduation, I'd love to start
a company in the skin care industry.

00:02:12.526 --> 00:02:14.143
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you both.

00:02:14.143 --> 00:02:18.709
So let's get started with some
questions submitted by the audience.

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:23.182
So the first one, did you come to school
planning to work outside of tech or

00:02:23.182 --> 00:02:26.870
did you change your
plans after you arrived?

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.590
JB, you want to take that one first off.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.296
&gt;&gt; Okay, yeah.

00:02:29.296 --> 00:02:33.975
My interest was very simple because I
was very deliberate what I wanted to do.

00:02:33.975 --> 00:02:38.270
Working entertainment for six years,
I was ready to see more industries, and

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:41.185
I figure the best way to do that
was to go to business school and

00:02:41.185 --> 00:02:43.945
then [COUGH] work in consulting because
they work with many different industries.

00:02:43.945 --> 00:02:48.079
So I was very focused and
knew what I wanted to do going into it.

00:02:48.079 --> 00:02:51.895
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was also very focused on
wanting to start a consumer company.

00:02:51.895 --> 00:02:55.493
What I was really excited about was
just being around the tech industry and

00:02:55.493 --> 00:02:56.770
around Silicon Valley and

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.736
just understanding the different ways
that people think here on the West Coast.

00:03:00.736 --> 00:03:03.832
So while I was never really interested
in going directly into tech,

00:03:03.832 --> 00:03:06.128
I was interested in getting
the tech kind of vibe.

00:03:06.128 --> 00:03:07.403
&gt;&gt; Great, and for me,

00:03:07.403 --> 00:03:11.903
very passionate about energy after
working at Sunrun Solar Company and

00:03:11.903 --> 00:03:17.014
now exploring ways to go and work in clean
energy back in Kenya, where I'm from.

00:03:17.014 --> 00:03:18.774
Been in the US quite a few years now so

00:03:18.774 --> 00:03:22.720
I want to use the knowledge I gain here at
the GSB to go back and do something there.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.348
&gt;&gt; So number two,

00:03:25.348 --> 00:03:30.576
what resources have the GSB provided you
with to explore life outside of tech?

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:31.931
Jessica, do you have a-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:03:31.931 --> 00:03:35.317
in starting a company,
Stanford is great at providing resources

00:03:35.317 --> 00:03:38.620
that are related to
entrepreneurial pursuits.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.750
Two things that come to mind
are Venture Studio, which is actually

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:46.130
a physical space on campus that you can
work on your company that you're starting.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:49.120
But there's also a huge
community of people who

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.210
are alumni of Stanford who were part
of Venture Studio while they were here.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:55.612
And they ask questions of each other and
we've kind of provided resources for

00:03:55.612 --> 00:03:56.197
each other.

00:03:56.197 --> 00:04:00.311
In addition, classes like Startup Garage
offer a hands-on experience and

00:04:00.311 --> 00:04:04.500
a really agnostic to what industry
you're wanting to start a company in.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.469
So they have a huge focus
on providing resources for

00:04:07.469 --> 00:04:11.763
entrepreneurial ventures, and
it doesn't matter if it's tech or not.

00:04:11.763 --> 00:04:13.670
&gt;&gt; Okay, JB?

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so even though I knew
I wanted to do consulting,

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:20.410
I still went to a number of those CMC,
Career Management Center, events.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.320
And they have a lot of different events
to help you just explore who you are, and

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.730
explore what you're interested in.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.140
It gives you ideas of what to get into.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.850
So I knew I wanted to do consulting,
but I said, you know what?

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:30.590
Let me try these out, and
they're actually pretty helpful.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:32.670
I noticed they help out a lot of people.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.150
It helped me even focus
more on consulting.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:35.940
So, CMC is a great resource, too.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:38.640
&gt;&gt; And for me, a bit like Jessica,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.270
I've had a chance to be part of
Venture CO, exploring my own venture.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.176
And also taking a couple of classes,
what we call across the street classes,

00:04:46.176 --> 00:04:47.993
in other schools here at Stanford.

00:04:47.993 --> 00:04:51.356
So I had a great opportunity to take
a energy entrepreneurship class last

00:04:51.356 --> 00:04:54.400
quarter, and really progress
the venture that I'm working on.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.440
&gt;&gt; Great, we'll move on to the next one.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.410
What academic extracurricular internship
experiences are helping you explore and

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:04.040
pursue careers outside of tech?

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:05.959
JB, I'll throw that one over to you.

00:05:05.959 --> 00:05:10.375
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, having a liberal arts
degree and not knowing business speak,

00:05:10.375 --> 00:05:14.800
as I'll call it, just going to business
school in general is very helpful.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.090
Just the basic,
the introductory strategy class, and

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.870
learning how people in
the traditional business world speak.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.520
That alone has been very helpful, and then
the different clubs and stuff as well.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.103
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say, too.

00:05:27.103 --> 00:05:29.183
Yeah, I mean, for me I'm kind of similar.

00:05:29.183 --> 00:05:33.761
I had a Human Biology degree, so
to me really getting the hardcore business

00:05:33.761 --> 00:05:38.431
skills has been incredibly helpful as I
think about what I'm going to do next.

00:05:38.431 --> 00:05:41.383
And then extracurricularly,
I'm getting a chance to head up

00:05:41.383 --> 00:05:45.301
the Stanford Energy Club, which I think
will be a great leadership experience and

00:05:45.301 --> 00:05:47.820
help me plug into the energy
community even deeper.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:50.630
&gt;&gt; All right, that's the next one.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:53.620
Can you talk about the social
aspects of the community?

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.312
Does it feel like everyone
is only interested in tech?

00:05:57.312 --> 00:05:58.159
Jessica, that's it?

00:05:58.159 --> 00:06:00.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Absolutely not.

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.310
One of my favorite things about
the GSB is how different we all are.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:07.417
And of course, we are extremely
supportive of each other, but

00:06:07.417 --> 00:06:11.594
seeing where people come from and where
they're going, there's really no focus.

00:06:11.594 --> 00:06:15.100
I think there's probably a slight
focus to wanting to start a company or

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.667
be part of an early-stage startup,
but from an industry perspective,

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:20.290
people are all over the place.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:22.220
And I'm really excited
to see where people go.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.301
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would just have to second that,

00:06:24.301 --> 00:06:28.850
especially coming from the entertainment
industry, which is definitely different.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.142
I know a number of people
who have even come to me.

00:06:31.142 --> 00:06:32.440
Actually, I'm a VP for

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:36.510
the Arts, Media &amp; Entertainment Club,
who want to get into the business.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:40.032
I know firsthand how difficult it is to
get in there because it's just so opaque.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.344
So yeah, l would just have to second that.

00:06:41.344 --> 00:06:43.794
Everyone is interested in so
many different things.

00:06:43.794 --> 00:06:46.856
&gt;&gt; Absolutely grand, so
that's incredibly helpful for

00:06:46.856 --> 00:06:48.725
people breaking into media MBA.

00:06:48.725 --> 00:06:50.901
Okay, great.

00:06:50.901 --> 00:06:55.220
Let's see, next we have a question a
little bit more about academics in class.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:59.370
So how are the industries
incorporated into class discussions?

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.047
What classes do this really well,
and do you have a favorite case?

00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:04.687
So JB?

00:07:04.687 --> 00:07:08.416
&gt;&gt; Overall, I think many different
classes do this very well actually.

00:07:08.416 --> 00:07:11.315
I think we should all answer it.

00:07:11.315 --> 00:07:12.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.730
&gt;&gt; My favorite case came
from our marketing class.

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:20.010
It was actually a case on Unilever
in Brazil which is they create soap.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.630
So you wouldn't think it's very
interesting, but it was in a sense

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:26.520
because they were trying to figure out
a way to break into a certain market.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.830
And we were in different groups,
and you have to do a whole report.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.880
And it was just cool seeing how all
the different solutions each group

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.980
came up with and then presented.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:37.090
So that was a very fun case,
and obviously not tech related.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:38.759
&gt;&gt; At all.

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:40.712
&gt;&gt; At all.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:40.712 --> 00:07:43.340
&gt;&gt; My favorite class has always
been formation of new ventures.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:44.730
I don't know if you guys have taken it.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.920
But the premise is every class,
we have a different case.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.512
And it's across all industries,
healthcare, financials, tech, consumer.

00:07:52.512 --> 00:07:55.880
And we diagnose what happens in a startup.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.040
And each class, the founder comes in and

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:01.770
talks to us and provides their
remarks throughout the class as well.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:06.279
And so probably my favorite case for that
was a company called Tiny Prints, which

00:08:06.279 --> 00:08:11.205
does cards customized and at the time that
something that wasn't available to people.

00:08:11.205 --> 00:08:14.628
And hearing the founders and
how they bootstrapped it and

00:08:14.628 --> 00:08:18.486
how they built it from the ground
up was really inspiring for me.

00:08:18.486 --> 00:08:23.256
And for me, similarly,
with regards to having a class one that

00:08:23.256 --> 00:08:27.867
stands out is my HR class where we did
a case on a company in Kenya called

00:08:27.867 --> 00:08:32.426
Juhudi Kilimo that did loans to
farmers to increase productivity.

00:08:32.426 --> 00:08:36.996
And they finally came in to talk to
us about issues about incentivizing

00:08:36.996 --> 00:08:41.265
workers and their field staff to
go out there and get in business.

00:08:41.265 --> 00:08:42.500
And that was fascinating.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:45.970
And for me, as someone who comes from
abroad, it's really cool to see how they

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.590
integrate cases from all over
the world into many of these classes.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.130
&gt;&gt; Great, let's see, next question.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.230
How have you kept yourself from
being pressured into tech with

00:08:58.230 --> 00:08:59.970
Silicon Valley so close by?

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:02.615
Definitely, there is a pull there, so-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:09:02.615 --> 00:09:03.200
&gt;&gt; Jessica?

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.291
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Stanford does a great job
at two things, well, many things.

00:09:07.291 --> 00:09:11.213
But two in particular are reflection and
prioritization.

00:09:11.213 --> 00:09:15.026
So in the very beginning of the year
we had a session about priorities, and

00:09:15.026 --> 00:09:17.160
setting your priorities.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:20.880
And really making sure that you're
focusing on your priorities, rather than

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.590
being pulled into the community think and
what you think everyone else is doing.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.290
So that's definitely bled
through my entire experience.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:32.188
I think also on reflection, making sure
that you're in tune with yourself and

00:09:32.188 --> 00:09:33.498
what you want to be doing.

00:09:33.498 --> 00:09:37.178
It's something that is known as
something that people do here.

00:09:37.178 --> 00:09:41.880
So I think that that has
really kept me on my track.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.480
And I haven't really swayed from it
because of those two activities.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:46.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I completely agree.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:49.840
And one thing I'd also add,
too, is because Stanford is so

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.510
close to Silicon Valley, and
there is some slight tech focus,

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:56.750
you get to see how tech has
entered other industries.

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:59.000
Just because you're not going to
go into the tech industry doesn't

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.700
mean technology's not changing industries.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.070
So it's actually very cool
to understand that, okay,

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:05.150
I might not go into tech itself.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:06.530
I might not go to work at Google or Apple,

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:09.800
but technology is changing
all different industries.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:10.770
And it's good to see that and

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:14.350
keep that in the back of your mind as
you figure out what you want to do.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:14.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:18.860
And on that point, again that opportunity
to maybe step across to other schools and

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.270
take a CS class.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:26.437
A lot of business students will do that
to get that grounding in the tech field.

00:10:26.437 --> 00:10:33.160
&gt;&gt; Great, so what resources are available
to help you pursue a career shift?

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:34.836
Okay, well I can take, you want to go?

00:10:34.836 --> 00:10:38.028
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think, I mean, for me,
the CMC are incredibly helpful,

00:10:38.028 --> 00:10:39.971
that's the Career Management Center.

00:10:39.971 --> 00:10:42.479
You can go there,
they have a ton of resources,

00:10:42.479 --> 00:10:46.369
they have a ton of job kind of advisors,
so that I think is a big one.

00:10:46.369 --> 00:10:48.392
JB?
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was also just going to add,

00:10:48.392 --> 00:10:52.078
GMC and the student clubs too,
I joined the Consulting Club.

00:10:52.078 --> 00:10:56.510
I remember consulting, recruiting
as an undergrad a little bit, and

00:10:56.510 --> 00:10:58.840
thought that was just too difficult.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.410
But the consulting club, and so
there are many different clubs, but

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:04.370
they give you the resources you need,
and the practice you need.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:06.570
And then because everyone
at the GSB comes from so

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:10.930
many different backgrounds that the
students here are very helpful as well,

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.180
if you're looking to change your career.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:15.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah, just think if you have
a picture of that, being able to,

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:20.250
in the first quarter, have 30 minute
coffee chats with people from, we

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:23.700
have 400 people in our class and I want to
learn about energy or entertainment.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.371
We have 30 minutes and

00:11:25.371 --> 00:11:30.059
that really gives also deep dive
really quickly and efficiently.

00:11:30.059 --> 00:11:35.476
Right, what is a typical day like at
the GSB, if there is indeed a typical day?

00:11:35.476 --> 00:11:37.178
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Jessica,

00:11:37.178 --> 00:11:39.620
do you have a day you could talk about?

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah, they're very different.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.246
I would say during the week we're in
classes, it depends on the day, but

00:11:46.246 --> 00:11:49.824
probably average of three classes a day,
three to four classes a day.

00:11:49.824 --> 00:11:55.281
So we spend a lot of class time, and
we also kind of interspersed with that

00:11:55.281 --> 00:12:01.376
have lunch or coffee with people who we're
trying to get to know or catch up with.

00:12:01.376 --> 00:12:04.512
For dinner, there's often meetings
where speakers come in, and

00:12:04.512 --> 00:12:07.484
you kind of go to that speaker,
and there's dinner provided.

00:12:07.484 --> 00:12:10.593
One of my favorite things
about the GSB is Talk,

00:12:10.593 --> 00:12:13.859
which is tonight,
every Wednesday night for us,

00:12:13.859 --> 00:12:18.740
where one student gives a 30-minute
kind of talk about his or her life.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:21.060
So that's a good community aspect.

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:25.780
I would say every day has an element of
academic social community and career.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:26.978
And it's really exciting.

00:12:26.978 --> 00:12:29.028
No day is the same, and
they all go very quickly.

00:12:29.028 --> 00:12:31.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah, JB?

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:35.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the only thing I would say is
typical is you know you're going to

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:38.540
have classes at the same time,
like Monday, Friday, or Tuesday, Thursday.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:41.520
And then your calendar, you get
invited to lots of different things.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:44.860
And it just starts to fill up, especially
if you like to say yes, which you should,

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:46.539
because you get to see lots
of different experiences.

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:48.725
So yeah, there's really no typical day.

00:12:48.725 --> 00:12:49.325
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:49.325 --> 00:12:52.113
&gt;&gt; I don't know about you two, but I find
my calendar pretty much owns my life.

00:12:52.113 --> 00:12:52.744
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:52.744 --> 00:12:53.859
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So I know that I'm there.

00:12:53.859 --> 00:12:57.221
&gt;&gt; I'm working on-
&gt;&gt; And then we have our social events,

00:12:57.221 --> 00:13:00.747
which is a great time to
get the classwork done for

00:13:00.747 --> 00:13:03.680
the following week, and prep for that.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:09.358
&gt;&gt; Okay, will you talk about
the global experiences you've had or

00:13:09.358 --> 00:13:11.049
plan on pursuing?

00:13:11.049 --> 00:13:13.660
&gt;&gt; Okay, I can take that one.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:17.274
As a former athlete, I got a chance
to travel all over the world but

00:13:17.274 --> 00:13:21.748
unfortunately, well, it was incredibly
fortunate that on those trips it would

00:13:21.748 --> 00:13:23.881
oftentimes just be a pool and a hotel.

00:13:23.881 --> 00:13:28.747
So being very nice for me just having
recently gone on a global experience

00:13:28.747 --> 00:13:33.070
trip through the GSB to South Africa
with 30 of my classmates.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:35.913
And we really got to explore the country,

00:13:35.913 --> 00:13:37.906
talk to some incredible
leaders down there.

00:13:37.906 --> 00:13:41.610
So that's been a new travel experience for
me.

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:45.675
And I'm hoping I can do a few more
of those during my time here.

00:13:45.675 --> 00:13:48.835
&gt;&gt; Yeah, similarly, I went on a global
study trip to Singapore and Indonesia.

00:13:48.835 --> 00:13:51.545
And just so you know the global
study trips are 30 students,

00:13:51.545 --> 00:13:55.155
and you're meeting every day with
business leaders, and government leaders.

00:13:55.155 --> 00:13:58.985
It's a really great way to get to
know a country besides the tourism.

00:13:58.985 --> 00:14:02.017
So I did that, and
I'm also doing an internship at London for

00:14:02.017 --> 00:14:04.573
four weeks over the summer
which will be great, and

00:14:04.573 --> 00:14:07.683
then looking into planning
a global study trip for next year.

00:14:07.683 --> 00:14:11.975
So the GSB definitely does a great
job at intermingling your kind of

00:14:11.975 --> 00:14:15.341
global experiences with
your academic pursuits.

00:14:15.341 --> 00:14:18.445
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean the global
experience is a requirement so

00:14:18.445 --> 00:14:20.410
you have to do some global effort.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.531
I did a global study trip to Japan
last winter, which was just amazing.

00:14:23.531 --> 00:14:27.440
And applying for a GMIX,
so we'll see if I get it.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:30.100
So hopefully four weeks somewhere abroad.

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:34.018
&gt;&gt; And you want to explain
a little bit about what a GMIX is?

00:14:34.018 --> 00:14:37.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so GMIX is basically,
from the way I see it,

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.420
we have 14 weeks I think it is,
yeah, 14 weeks off for summer.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:41.600
It's a mini internship so

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:44.620
instead of a ten-week or eight-week
internship, generally around four weeks.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:45.808
It can be flexible depending
on how much time you have.

00:14:45.808 --> 00:14:49.374
Where you get like a stipend to live
in a certain country and work for

00:14:49.374 --> 00:14:53.560
a company, just really get to
immerse yourself in the experience.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.861
&gt;&gt; It's a really great way to have like a
little snapshot into what it would be like

00:14:56.861 --> 00:14:59.124
to work abroad or
in that particular industry, too.

00:14:59.124 --> 00:15:01.338
It's a good way just to try it out for
four weeks.

00:15:01.338 --> 00:15:01.958
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:15:01.958 --> 00:15:06.763
&gt;&gt; The next question,
what exposure do you have to clean tech,

00:15:06.763 --> 00:15:10.357
renewables, energy efficiency at the GSB?

00:15:10.357 --> 00:15:13.463
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I suppose this one's for me.
So there are some GSB classes that

00:15:13.463 --> 00:15:18.061
are focused on clean energy
development and demands.

00:15:18.061 --> 00:15:21.436
And then, of course,
the engineering department,

00:15:21.436 --> 00:15:26.840
there's a ton of classes you can take,
and design your schedule that way.

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:30.123
And a lot of people interested in
environment can also do a dual degree.

00:15:30.123 --> 00:15:33.331
It's called a EI degree and
you stay on for an extra quarter.

00:15:33.331 --> 00:15:35.916
And it evens dives deeper
into those subjects.

00:15:38.056 --> 00:15:41.972
How does social impact and corporate
social responsibility emerge in your GSB

00:15:41.972 --> 00:15:44.415
experience, whether inside or
outside class?

00:15:47.458 --> 00:15:50.238
Question for you-
&gt;&gt; I know ethics was one of my favorite

00:15:50.238 --> 00:15:52.201
classes first quarter, and so

00:15:52.201 --> 00:15:57.150
that's a class where you're really
dealing with the difficult questions.

00:15:57.150 --> 00:16:02.110
Now the way it's taught, the teacher
does not tell you what's right or wrong.

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:05.290
They just have you,
they talk about business situations,

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:10.320
like the Libor scandal I forgot what
Intel was talking about changing

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:17.406
their supply chain to have safer minerals
and it's basically worked on their own.

00:16:17.406 --> 00:16:21.979
And again, so that's definitely a class
that focuses on those as people have those

00:16:21.979 --> 00:16:24.882
discussions, because I do
think it's very important.

00:16:24.882 --> 00:16:27.810
&gt;&gt; And also there's
a Social Innovation Club here.

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:31.628
The Energy Club, actually,
we collaborate with them sometimes,

00:16:31.628 --> 00:16:33.357
they do a big social innovation retreat.

00:16:33.357 --> 00:16:37.110
So it's certainly there's a community
you can plug into in a big way.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:40.586
&gt;&gt; There are also opportunities to be
a part of actual companies who are doing

00:16:40.586 --> 00:16:42.392
social good and consulting for them.

00:16:42.392 --> 00:16:47.189
And it's a pretty time-intensive thing but
those people are learning a lot.

00:16:47.189 --> 00:16:50.460
&gt;&gt; And the last thing I want to add and
I haven't quite done it yet but

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:54.102
you can talk to your professors in class,
so you can challenge them and

00:16:54.102 --> 00:16:56.050
ask them certain questions.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:58.863
I only bring this up because
I'm listening to an audio book

00:16:58.863 --> 00:17:02.811
about the financialization of America and
it's talking certain things.

00:17:02.811 --> 00:17:05.428
I remember learning certain
things in finance and

00:17:05.428 --> 00:17:07.552
I know in the future if I hear something.

00:17:07.552 --> 00:17:10.856
This is a little jargon, you can challenge
your professors and be like why is this?

00:17:10.856 --> 00:17:12.396
I heard this and I heard that and

00:17:12.396 --> 00:17:15.663
it can actually foster very good
discussions actually, right?

00:17:15.663 --> 00:17:19.070
&gt;&gt; Excellent,
how supportive is the student body?

00:17:19.070 --> 00:17:22.540
Do you feel that it is a competitive
environment or supportive community?

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:25.717
Jessica, do you have a good-
&gt;&gt; I have a very strong opinion on this.

00:17:25.717 --> 00:17:27.570
It's incredibly supportive.

00:17:27.570 --> 00:17:31.340
I have never been in
a community of people that is,

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:33.900
we're full of 400 cheerleaders
by our sides to help us out.

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:37.790
I've never felt someone is
trying going to ahead and

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:41.110
do something without bringing
the whole class behind them.

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:44.398
I think some significant examples
are in the first quarter,

00:17:44.398 --> 00:17:47.946
in the second quarter when some
people had finance backgrounds or

00:17:47.946 --> 00:17:51.442
accounting backgrounds would just
to help out and for nothing.

00:17:51.442 --> 00:17:56.435
Just spend many hours trying to help
their classmates who came from liberal

00:17:56.435 --> 00:18:00.492
arts backgrounds and
it's really just a community of help.

00:18:00.492 --> 00:18:03.810
And people know that I'm interested
in starting a skin care company and

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:06.863
people have reached out to me to
want to share their experiences.

00:18:06.863 --> 00:18:09.711
And it's really a very,
very special community and

00:18:09.711 --> 00:18:12.051
I don't think there's anything like it.

00:18:12.051 --> 00:18:14.320
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I totally second that.

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:17.867
And yeah, if you want someone to
help you out with the contacts,

00:18:17.867 --> 00:18:22.154
the industry they're interested in,
people are just so willing to do that.

00:18:22.154 --> 00:18:26.136
And I'm just thinking only sometimes when
you work in a group project maybe you

00:18:26.136 --> 00:18:29.536
don't have a stronger background
that someone else on your team.

00:18:29.536 --> 00:18:31.609
I just find people so
willing to help coach and

00:18:31.609 --> 00:18:33.638
bring you up to speed
in those experiences.

00:18:33.638 --> 00:18:35.660
So it's simply amazing.

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:40.600
&gt;&gt; And I specifically did recruiting for
consulting, which can scare some people,

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:43.400
because generally business schools
are feeders into consulting.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.784
And one, it was nice that Stanford has
such a diversed background that it

00:18:46.784 --> 00:18:49.296
wasn't the, again,
I'm remembering undergrad.

00:18:49.296 --> 00:18:52.389
There's 200 people in one room,
all fighting for ten jobs.

00:18:52.389 --> 00:18:55.943
Stanford didn't feel that way at all,
because one is 40 people and

00:18:55.943 --> 00:18:58.048
then people are just so collaborative.

00:18:58.048 --> 00:19:00.527
So even in the intense
recruiting of it all,

00:19:00.527 --> 00:19:04.818
Stanford is still an incredibly
collaborative and supportive community.

00:19:04.818 --> 00:19:07.638
&gt;&gt; And one thing to bring up on
that is that we have a norm of

00:19:07.638 --> 00:19:09.100
non-grade disclosures.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:10.067
So that also helps out.

00:19:10.067 --> 00:19:14.317
People kind of are on the spectrum of
being super focused on academics and

00:19:14.317 --> 00:19:16.766
also prioritizing other things as well.

00:19:16.766 --> 00:19:22.880
And so not competing for the number one or
number two spot, it's not relevant for us.

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:25.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and allowing us to take risks and
may be take a class that is,

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:28.770
we don't have a strong background in and
we can really try to push ourselves.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:33.820
What networking opportunities
available to non-tech students?

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:36.487
&gt;&gt; I can talk about
through the Energy Club.

00:19:36.487 --> 00:19:41.887
We host a careers event in collaboration
with the Stanford Energy Club every year.

00:19:41.887 --> 00:19:48.680
We're bringing in speakers, we have
brown bag lunches throughout the year.

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:51.310
So we try to do that through the club.

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:52.988
I don't know.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would echo that for

00:19:52.988 --> 00:19:54.000
the Retail Club.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:56.410
Obviously, as you can tell,

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:59.882
we have clubs that go kind of by industry
in addition to other activities and

00:19:59.882 --> 00:20:02.976
one thing that the clubs do,
at least the Retail Club, is a trek.

00:20:02.976 --> 00:20:07.601
So there was a trek to Europe but the one
that I went on was to San Francisco, so

00:20:07.601 --> 00:20:09.135
it was half an hour away.

00:20:09.135 --> 00:20:13.668
I'm meeting with various companies, I'm
really getting to know people within those

00:20:13.668 --> 00:20:18.087
companies and networking across retails
systems, for Chubbies and other places.

00:20:20.962 --> 00:20:25.849
&gt;&gt; How invested as GSB in
providing recruiting resources for

00:20:25.849 --> 00:20:30.846
students who are interested
in careers outside of tech?.

00:20:30.846 --> 00:20:32.150
&gt;&gt; What a similar question, I don't know-
&gt;&gt; I think

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:32.738
we've touched on this a little bit.

00:20:32.738 --> 00:20:36.659
But again, the CMC and the clubs do such
an amazing job of giving you the resources

00:20:36.659 --> 00:20:38.690
you would need no matter what.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:42.020
Again, we talked about the treks, we
talked about the CMC and on the different,

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:45.050
you can use individual one-on-one
meetings if you're a CMC advisor,

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:46.820
they can help you reach out to alumni.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:51.540
There's plenty of resources
that will help you and

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:54.481
can find jobs outside of tech.

00:20:54.481 --> 00:20:55.227
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:20:55.227 --> 00:20:55.952
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Right,

00:20:55.952 --> 00:20:59.606
can you speak a bit about any
experience you might have meeting

00:20:59.606 --> 00:21:02.698
students at GSB who come
from a nonprofit background

00:21:02.698 --> 00:21:06.861
who are interested in focusing on
nonprofits later in their careers?

00:21:06.861 --> 00:21:12.720
&gt;&gt; Personally, I have a lot of friends
who come from nonprofit backgrounds,

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:18.490
and a couple that comes to mind actually
starting a company in this space.

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:24.010
And so Stanford is incredibly supportive
for people who want to get into nonprofit.

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:27.490
There's also funding you can access for
your summer to work with

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:32.470
nonprofit companies and then there's also
a fellowship beyond, once you graduate

00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:35.970
that you can access, if you're going to
be working in a nonprofit career.

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:38.691
So the GSB is incredibly supportive.

00:21:38.691 --> 00:21:40.115
Anything else to add on that one?

00:21:40.115 --> 00:21:43.134
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Is it common for

00:21:43.134 --> 00:21:48.202
student's post-grad goals to change
over the course of the program?

00:21:48.202 --> 00:21:48.912
&gt;&gt; Definitely,

00:21:48.912 --> 00:21:52.338
I think a lot of people come in not
really knowing what they want to do,

00:21:52.338 --> 00:21:56.990
knowing that they want to change fields
but want to explore different hypotheses.

00:21:56.990 --> 00:21:58.285
I think it'll be interesting,
we're all first years.

00:21:58.285 --> 00:22:02.234
So it'll be interesting to see what
happens after the summer where a lot of

00:22:02.234 --> 00:22:06.312
people have internships and they test
out the hypothesis of do they like it or

00:22:06.312 --> 00:22:08.250
do they not or various aspects.

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:12.460
And then come back in the fall
ready to do full-time recruiting or

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:14.140
go back to the company
that they interned at.

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:15.360
It'll be interesting to see.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:18.500
&gt;&gt; And it's currently almost May,
we're a week away from May, and

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:21.800
I know a number of people who still
don't have internships for the summer.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:23.700
And it's not because they
can't get internships,

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:26.890
its because they're still just really
trying to figure out what they want to do.

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:29.720
So even right now, some people are still
trying to figure out where to explore.

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:31.780
&gt;&gt; We're waiting for the perfect-
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:33.915
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've had heard from the second
years. This summer can be a big time,

00:22:33.915 --> 00:22:37.571
because it really helps refine how
you approach the second year and

00:22:37.571 --> 00:22:39.408
really hanging on something.

00:22:39.408 --> 00:22:44.223
&gt;&gt; Knowing you wanted to enter a non-tech
field before entering business school,

00:22:44.223 --> 00:22:45.650
why did you choose GSB?

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:48.053
&gt;&gt; I can talk about that one.

00:22:48.053 --> 00:22:51.727
GSB, it was definitely my top
choice when I lived in Los Angeles.

00:22:51.727 --> 00:22:54.372
So even though I grew up in the Northeast,

00:22:54.372 --> 00:22:58.906
it would be nice to stand in the West
Coast and I'm doing this whole thing

00:22:58.906 --> 00:23:02.480
short term, I might want to
get back as a media long term.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:07.403
But what I was saying earlier, this is how
technology is changing every industry.

00:23:07.403 --> 00:23:11.809
So I wanted to be nearby the tech
space specifically in media.

00:23:11.809 --> 00:23:14.790
I mean,
Facebook is becoming a media company.

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:18.480
Apple is having a much bigger
space in the media space,

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:19.750
bigger play in the media space.

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:22.090
So these tech companies are becoming
media companies from my point of

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:22.746
view, in a sense.

00:23:22.746 --> 00:23:29.100
So I just wanted to be around that
sort of life, I guess, in proximity.

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:31.393
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I agree.
I wanted to be around the big ideas and

00:23:31.393 --> 00:23:36.149
ideas that are changing the world and
just open this, a new ways of thinking and

00:23:36.149 --> 00:23:39.272
collaborating, and
the GSB was definitely that.

00:23:39.272 --> 00:23:43.412
Beyond that, I think the community
that we have all talked about is just

00:23:43.412 --> 00:23:47.152
unparalleled and that's really
what made GSB my first choice.

00:23:47.152 --> 00:23:50.315
And then I'd also say that if
you want to start a company,

00:23:50.315 --> 00:23:53.710
those resources are unparalleled as well.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:58.266
I think it's a fantastic place to start
a company regardless of what industry you

00:23:58.266 --> 00:23:59.343
want to started in.

00:23:59.343 --> 00:24:02.248
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, yeah and, I was lucky enough
to do my undergraduates here, so-

00:24:02.248 --> 00:24:03.050
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:04.982
&gt;&gt; Any time [CROSSTALK] farmer,

00:24:04.982 --> 00:24:06.032
I'll take it.

00:24:06.032 --> 00:24:10.491
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] How much does your first and

00:24:10.491 --> 00:24:15.044
second year schedules allow for
you to be in extracurriculars?

00:24:15.044 --> 00:24:18.154
Is it recommended to do
just one extracurricular,

00:24:18.154 --> 00:24:23.091
like take a new leadership role or would
you be able to participate in a few clubs?

00:24:23.091 --> 00:24:26.698
&gt;&gt; So clubs here are different from,
at least my undergrad experience, in that

00:24:26.698 --> 00:24:31.080
they don't really dominate your social
experience if you don't want them to.

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:34.905
So we have something called,
we have the first five weeks.

00:24:34.905 --> 00:24:38.906
Basically, we can't be apart of club and
that's in an effort to get back in

00:24:38.906 --> 00:24:42.928
the swing of things of being a student and
getting to know your classmates.

00:24:42.928 --> 00:24:46.589
And so by the time you join, it's actually
the midway through your first quarter.

00:24:46.589 --> 00:24:50.244
And I don't know about you guys,
but I joined several.

00:24:50.244 --> 00:24:54.022
The reason our club being one of them,
but also just actively,

00:24:54.022 --> 00:24:56.671
it's like the Wine Circle and
the Eat Club.

00:24:56.671 --> 00:24:58.111
And so, throughout your first year,

00:24:58.111 --> 00:25:00.003
you can definitely be
involved in a bunch of them.

00:25:00.003 --> 00:25:03.348
And then, if you want to take
on a leadership position,

00:25:03.348 --> 00:25:06.492
it's definitely manageable,
and I'm doing one.

00:25:06.492 --> 00:25:07.572
It's the Eating Club.

00:25:07.572 --> 00:25:08.985
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:08.985 --> 00:25:10.722
&gt;&gt; But it's definitely manageable.

00:25:10.722 --> 00:25:11.762
It's kind of like,

00:25:11.762 --> 00:25:15.319
whatever you want to prioritize is
where you should spend your time.

00:25:15.319 --> 00:25:16.003
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would agree.

00:25:16.003 --> 00:25:19.316
I mean, in terms of club leadership,
I think, it really depends.

00:25:19.316 --> 00:25:23.120
One, how much the club does over the
course of the year, how big a role it is,

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:25.031
how much you want to bring to the role.

00:25:25.031 --> 00:25:28.902
I do see some people take on maybe more
than one leadership role in a club,

00:25:28.902 --> 00:25:30.740
but I think typically it's one and

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:34.350
then being a member or
playing a supporting role in the club.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:36.906
&gt;&gt; But I've definitely seen
like some second years.

00:25:36.906 --> 00:25:39.710
They're done now, but like who, we had
a couple of clubs, one or two clubs.

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:40.414
They did Arbuckle.

00:25:40.414 --> 00:25:43.298
I met someone who was like an Arbuckle
fellow, and also saw [LAUGH] and

00:25:43.298 --> 00:25:44.520
like planned a gimmick too.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:45.021
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:45.021 --> 00:25:49.204
&gt;&gt; Yeah, they were very, very, very busy
because that is a lot, but it's doable.

00:25:49.204 --> 00:25:49.918
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:25:51.733 --> 00:25:55.837
&gt;&gt; With a non-tech bias, how does
the GSB prepare you for starting for

00:25:55.837 --> 00:25:59.150
profit businesses in Africa or
low income countries?

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:01.018
&gt;&gt; This is fun for me.

00:26:01.018 --> 00:26:05.611
[LAUGH] So, I mean, there have been
quite a few businesses started in

00:26:05.611 --> 00:26:08.123
these countries by alums of the GSB.

00:26:08.123 --> 00:26:13.617
So I have plugged into the network, people
who've done that in Kenya specifically.

00:26:13.617 --> 00:26:18.890
Again, there's a ton of resources,
people who've funded businesses overseas.

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:21.117
So there's a lot of potential.

00:26:21.117 --> 00:26:25.588
A lot of innovations have come out of
Stanford for work in low income countries.

00:26:25.588 --> 00:26:30.640
One I can think of specifically
came out of the d.school

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:36.139
was a low tech incubator for
babies in low income countries.

00:26:36.139 --> 00:26:39.843
So there's a lot of support here and
there's a lot of precedent for

00:26:39.843 --> 00:26:42.525
people who have done that
coming out of the GSB.

00:26:42.525 --> 00:26:44.050
[INAUDIBLE] more generally, actually.

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:48.240
&gt;&gt; Can you talk about your
experiences with faculty?

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:51.454
&gt;&gt; I've had great experience with faculty.

00:26:51.454 --> 00:26:55.345
And it's just funny thinking, comparing
it to my undergrad experience, right?

00:26:55.345 --> 00:27:00.366
Didn't really talk to faculty at all that
often, but I've had a couple of lunches.

00:27:00.366 --> 00:27:01.850
Accounting, I was terrified for

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:05.083
accounting because that was just a class
tat was going to be, this was going to

00:27:05.083 --> 00:27:08.590
be the toughest thing from a non-business
background, but I love the class.

00:27:08.590 --> 00:27:11.810
I had lunch with the teacher
while we were in class together.

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:13.760
I've had lunch with the teacher
again like after class.

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:16.720
So the faculty is just
incredibly approachable.

00:27:16.720 --> 00:27:21.515
We, because the way the school is
setup is the big right in the middle.

00:27:21.515 --> 00:27:25.047
So you see your teachers even like when
you're not in class with them anymore and

00:27:25.047 --> 00:27:27.012
like, it's just fun to catch up with them.

00:27:27.012 --> 00:27:29.607
So I've just had amazing
experiences with faculty members.

00:27:29.607 --> 00:27:32.229
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'd say they were
deeply supportive just like

00:27:32.229 --> 00:27:33.720
the community of us is.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:37.890
And so I had a question about a start up,
and I went in to talk to a professor for

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:42.030
office hours, and she spent way
longer than I expected her to or

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:45.710
to have time to because she really cared
about getting to the bottom of it and

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:46.860
problem solving with me.

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:48.799
And it's an ongoing conversation.

00:27:48.799 --> 00:27:52.179
So it's really actually incredible
how much they want to help us and

00:27:52.179 --> 00:27:54.750
want to see us succeed and
propel us into the world.

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:57.761
&gt;&gt; I second all of that, and I actually,
just last week, had an amazing experience

00:27:57.761 --> 00:28:00.304
where I got invited to go to an ice
hockey game with a faculty member.

00:28:00.304 --> 00:28:02.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We got to spend time watching

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:03.429
the San Jose Sharks.

00:28:03.429 --> 00:28:04.964
[LAUGH] Pretty incredible, so.

00:28:04.964 --> 00:28:07.516
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I've actually got more thing like

00:28:07.516 --> 00:28:10.999
there was one teacher who,
half way through the quarter,

00:28:10.999 --> 00:28:13.227
you do evaluation of your teachers.

00:28:13.227 --> 00:28:15.590
And one teacher, we didn't love him.

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:18.790
We gave him not a very strong evaluation.

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:21.530
But the way he responded
to it was incredible to me.

00:28:21.530 --> 00:28:25.854
He took it, and then he spent one class
literally just talking to us about what

00:28:25.854 --> 00:28:30.122
he could do better, how he can improve,
his experiences from prior years.

00:28:30.122 --> 00:28:35.077
And the second half of the class went so
much better because that teacher

00:28:35.077 --> 00:28:40.210
really cared about changing the way
he was teaching us, the engagement.

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:42.740
&gt;&gt; What support and
guidance do alumni provide?

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:45.460
&gt;&gt; I can take that.

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:50.052
I mean, I've had just really
amazing interactions with alumni,

00:28:50.052 --> 00:28:52.681
especially ones involved in energy.

00:28:52.681 --> 00:28:54.314
I've had a lot of conversations that way.

00:28:54.314 --> 00:28:58.841
There's actually a standard
professionals in energy group where

00:28:58.841 --> 00:29:03.546
they help set us up with mentors who
work in the field outside the GSB.

00:29:03.546 --> 00:29:08.204
So incredibly supportive alumni community,
I think that's one of

00:29:08.204 --> 00:29:12.628
the huge pluses of the GSB is that
community you get to plug into.

00:29:12.628 --> 00:29:16.012
&gt;&gt; I definitely think it's because
it's a much tighter community that you

00:29:16.012 --> 00:29:17.576
can really connect with alumni.

00:29:17.576 --> 00:29:20.600
[LAUGH] I remember, especially during
the recruiting process or consulting,

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:23.574
a [LAUGH] number of alumni were definitely
reaching out to me and saying, hey,

00:29:23.574 --> 00:29:24.920
you should think about this firm.

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:25.882
Our firm is pretty good.

00:29:25.882 --> 00:29:28.348
And so, I just had good
interactions with them as well.

00:29:28.348 --> 00:29:31.222
&gt;&gt; Yeah, people are incredibly
welcome to the emails and cold emails.

00:29:31.222 --> 00:29:33.637
If you have a Stanford address,

00:29:33.637 --> 00:29:38.140
they are definitely welcome
to answering your questions.

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:40.759
&gt;&gt; Great, so
we're almost out of time today.

00:29:40.759 --> 00:29:44.931
Before we go, JB and Jessica,
any final words of advice for

00:29:44.931 --> 00:29:48.860
people thinking about applying
to Stanford for an MBA?

00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:53.079
&gt;&gt; I would probably say, look into
the community that you're joining.

00:29:53.079 --> 00:29:57.233
I think Stanford is an incredible
community, but it is one that is a pretty

00:29:57.233 --> 00:30:01.789
intense community and one that really does
to open up and share with each other and

00:30:01.789 --> 00:30:03.809
get to know each other really well.

00:30:03.809 --> 00:30:05.220
I don't know if that's for everyone.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.800
It's definitely for me and
it works really well for me.

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:11.021
But just really understands
the community that you're joining and

00:30:11.021 --> 00:30:12.499
where you will play into that.

00:30:12.499 --> 00:30:15.459
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I would say, just be really
thoughtful about what you want to get

00:30:15.459 --> 00:30:18.466
out of the experience, but then be also
open at the same time because like,

00:30:18.466 --> 00:30:20.080
you understand you're going to change.

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:23.530
So come in with a goal,
like a loose sort of goal,

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.970
and leave yourself open to change.

00:30:25.970 --> 00:30:30.027
&gt;&gt; I guess my final comment would be if
you know you don't want to work in tech,

00:30:30.027 --> 00:30:34.294
there's plenty of other opportunities
that you can explore in the GSB side.

00:30:34.294 --> 00:30:37.720
So I don't think that should be a big
concern as you were thinking about

00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:38.774
Stanford for an MBA.

00:30:38.774 --> 00:30:42.024
It's an incredible experience for
me so far, yeah, and

00:30:42.024 --> 00:30:43.799
definitely seconded as well.

00:30:43.799 --> 00:30:49.340
&gt;&gt; Great, okay, well, that's it.

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:54.927
Thank you too for joining us and everyone
in the audience who are participating, you

00:30:54.927 --> 00:31:00.690
can learn more about the MBA program and
the admission process on the GSB website.

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:04.658
The application for
the next cycle will be available in June.

00:31:04.658 --> 00:31:07.558
Yeah, thanks again and
I hope you all have a great day.

